0d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285170 1111 #uuid "eed69bc8-4626-5a22-a503-c5fa984364d8" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285171 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285171 1111 #uuid "7d187bd0-7aca-5483-b493-1f8ed77da5c2" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285173 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1089 "q-162245" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1100 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $9$th roots of unity?" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1113 #uuid "8843b4c7-5e47-597f-9c2c-fd29812c3fb4" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285172 1145 "Geometrically, the $9$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular nonagon (regular $9$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png)" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285174 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285175 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285176 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285177 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285178 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1041 "selection" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285176 13194139534382 true] [17592186285173 1110 #uuid "49a80822-caa3-554e-972b-dfc326b58faf" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285174 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285174 1111 #uuid "19915621-0138-5923-be83-52bdd8ac3989" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285175 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/58013857f5024f78c322059e5ffdbf10943b0479722202c972a51ccda4be3525.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285175 1111 #uuid "43683992-a956-57e5-a99e-d2b8a7d3101e" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285176 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285176 1111 #uuid "0781535a-2dff-5587-ad41-92e404ca3d86" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285177 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285177 1111 #uuid "ca23e92c-b5e4-58ca-91e2-4fd7e3e52267" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285178 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/b473ada0488958a7faf410a6cadb0a1b9aedfde1925658a1f635913b1e2507c3.png" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534382 true] [17592186285178 1111 #uuid "1f1e8548-25d5-5908-b697-5e9e87dda942" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285180 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1089 "q-216709" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/bf78e90c844df2d80ffb3b08476ecd0370ffbbf63050bcabf20265692b2deeb3.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1113 #uuid "3ed5b8d5-3a7c-59e2-b811-f15ac112925f" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285179 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $9$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $9$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/e797056434dc122218bdc501cd9e1d5af93127658daa14f7c38b3bd7be8b877f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/9}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285181 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285182 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285183 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285184 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285185 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1041 "selection" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285184 13194139534382 true] [17592186285180 1110 #uuid "0fafb208-fdc0-5379-a02a-fd6fd38c13a0" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285181 1034 "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/9}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534382 true] [17592186285181 1111 #uuid "a0fc62e4-922d-5c84-968e-7c9aa30f931d" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285182 1034 "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534382 true] [17592186285182 1111 #uuid "6eccb878-10fd-5782-a89d-fe79f2df90c6" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285183 1034 "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534382 true] [17592186285183 1111 #uuid "752d9baf-25f3-5f35-89cb-7bf3b494e131" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285184 1034 "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534382 true] [17592186285184 1111 #uuid "f29ffb91-710b-5314-9fe3-81339f39922f" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285185 1034 "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/9}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534382 true] [17592186285185 1111 #uuid "4a6a5eec-7bbf-50a6-8ea6-9495bc1ce328" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285187 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1089 "q-216713" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/70665e4b48e324582ff9b1d448580d2e4462cddc7e51ac42a5766bb605d0bf1c.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1113 #uuid "653ed88c-debb-5ddb-9d18-ba57a77e805a" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285186 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $8$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $8$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{8}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/8}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/31a716f5f4abcda0baea7586f732f9009d2cd4821b429d060d985858c61a3b62.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{(3)\\pi \\text{i}/4}={e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285188 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285189 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285190 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285191 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285192 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1041 "selection" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285190 13194139534382 true] [17592186285187 1110 #uuid "c8a265cf-7daf-569c-8012-a454831bec59" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285188 1034 "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/8}" 13194139534382 true] [17592186285188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194